{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9066c897faeadc2c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"State verification complete:\n- package.json perfectly matches AC 1 and AC 2 (name nextmove, private true, workspaces [\"apps/*\"], packageManager \"npm@10\", engines.node \"22.x\", turbo ^2.5.0, scripts).\n- Test files: 4 tests. Test 1 = parse check; Test 2 = AC1 identity/workspaces; Test 3 = AC2 packageManager/engines; Test 4 = AC3 turbo + scripts. Note: The AC mapping is slightly off in the test file naming (the tests are labeled AC 1, 2, 3, but the actual PRD AC 3 is \"committed to git\", while the test's \"AC 3\" covers the turbo portion of PRD AC 2). That said, the coverage is there: all content of AC 1 is covered by test 2, the packageManager/engines part of AC 2 is covered by test 3, the turbo/scripts part of AC 2 is covered by test 4. AC 3 (committed with git exit 0) is verified at the git level, not in unit tests — which is acceptable for a scaffold story; the commit itself serves as the verification.\n- Marker is tracked at HEAD (empty, 0 bytes), and has been deleted from the working tree.\n- Untracked harness files are present and must not be committed.\n\nShould I also verify the test execution? The task states that the final message is the deliverable; it's a read-only plan. Running `node --test test/package.test.cjs` is read-only and safe, and lets me confirm green. However, the planner agent's tools are Read, Grep, Glob — no Bash. I've already used Bash once. The task says read-only verification is permitted. To be safe and complete, I'll run the test once (read-only).\n","signature":"b671c89785a044798e1de1ca004ed91e"}],"model":"unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":21187,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bb22f7d3bcb554e","session_id":"18cab620-6240-4ed3-9b04-d218b0f3b147","uuid":"99d7d9fc-ebec-4350-b4b3-bb24be