   - Test \"package.json exists and JSON parses\" — guards parseability (prerequisite for all ACs).\n     - Test \"AC 1: identity and workspaces\" — asserts `name`, `private`, `workspaces.includes(\"apps/*\")` (PRD AC 1).\n     - Test \"AC 2: pinned npm and Node 22 engines\" — asserts `packageManager === \"npm@10\"`, `engines.node === \"22.x\"` (PRD AC 1's `packageManager`/`engines` fields, labeled AC 2 in-file).\n     - Test \"AC 3: turbo 2.x devDependency and turbo-run scripts\" — asserts `devDependencies.turbo` is exactly `^2.5.0` and matches `/\\^?2\\./`, and all three scripts (PRD AC 2).\n   - Note: in-file labels are offset by one relative to the PRD (in-file \"AC 3\" covers PRD AC 2; PRD AC 3 — \"committed with git commands exiting 0\" — is verified by the git steps in Phase 2, not by a unit test). All content requirements are covered 1:1. No test changes required; renaming the in-file labels is optional and would modify a committed file, so do it only if the orchestrator wants it.\n\n### Phase 1: Run final verification commands\n\n3. **Run the required unit tests** (Working dir: repo root)\n   - Action: `node --test test/package.test.cjs` — expect `# pass 4`, `# fail 0`.\n   - Why: Test Strategy Gate for a pure-scaffold story; already verified green (4/4) in this analysis.\n   - Dependencies: Phase 0\n   - Risk: Low. Do **not** use `npm test`/`turbo run test` as the gate — turbo 2.x rejects the literal `\"npm@10\"` packageManager at runtime (known, accepted).\n\n4. **Verify the git facts** (read-only)\n   - Action:\n     - `git status --porcelain` — expect `D .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` plus the 7 untracked harness entries (and nothing else).\n     - `git ls-files | grep story-updates` — expect `.story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` (tracked).\n     - `git show HEAD:.story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed | wc -c` — expect `0` (empty marker in HEAD).\n     - `git log --oneline origin/iotahub/phase-1..HEAD` (if the remote-tracking ref exists) — expect the branch to be 7 commits ahead.\n   - Why: Confirms the closeout state assumed by Phase 2; also serves as the AC 3 evidence (files committed, git commands exiting 0).\n   - Dependencies: Phase 1 step 3\n   - Risk: Low. All four checks already executed and confirmed in this analysis.\n\n### Phase 2: Phase 5 closeout procedure (only mutating steps)\n\n5. **Restore the marker in the working tree** (File: `.story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed`)\n   - Action: `git restore -- .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed`\n   - Why: Prefer `git restore` over `touch` so the working-tree file is guaranteed byte-identical to HEAD (empty file). `touch` is an acceptable fallback only if `git restore` errors (e.g. pathspec mismatch).\n   - Dependencies: Step 4\n   - Risk: Low.\n\n6. **Verify the marker exists**\n   - Action: `ls .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` — expect the path to list; `git status --porcelain` no longer shows `D .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed`.\n   - Dependencies: Step 5\n   - Risk: Low.\n\n7. **Stage ONLY the marker path**\n   - Action: `git add .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed`\n   - Why: The restored file is byte-identical to HEAD (0 bytes), so this stages **nothing new** — the index will already match HEAD for that path. That is the expected outcome: the marker is already tracked in HEAD, and the commit in step 8 is expected to be a no-op. **Never use `git add .`** — it would stage the 7 untracked harness files (`.last-branch`, `.ralph/`, `.story-updates/processed/`, `prd.json.bak`, `ralph-summary.txt`, `ralph.log`), which must not be committed.\n   - Dependencies: Step 6\n   - Risk: Low. Guard: re-run `git status --porcelain` after `git add` and confirm the untracked harness files are still listed as `??` and no harness file appears as a staged (`A `/`M `) entry.\n\n8. **Commit**\n   - Action: `git commit -m \"chore: mark STORY-setup-001a as passed\"`\n   - Expected outcome (state the expectation explicitly): because the restored 